<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/expert/corporate-governance">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Corporate governance is <strong>the system that keeps those who run companies accountable to
    those who own them</strong> — and to the societies that license them. It exists because of
    the agency problem: the separation of ownership and control (Jensen and Meckling, 1976)
    creates permanent temptation — empire building, pet projects, quiet underperformance. The
    machinery is boards, audits, disclosure and incentives; scandals are its maintenance
    records. And although governance sounds abstract, its unit-level topics — motivation,
    organisation, recruitment and communication — are precisely where it operates: every
    governance failure on record is a failure in one of those four systems, dressed up in
    financial reporting.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the agency problem, the board, the tools, and the stakeholder debate" widget="equationStep" config={govCfg} />

  <h2>Motivating employees: incentives as the sharpest governance tool</h2>
  <p>
    Governance's first principle is that people follow their incentives, so incentive design
    <em>is</em> control — more powerful than any audit. Equity pay aligns managers with owners,
    but the alignment is directional, not exact: stock options and vesting schedules reward
    share-price movements, which can be manufactured by buybacks and cost-cutting as easily as
    by value creation. The measured cost: Lazonick's analyses found S&P 500 firms spent
    trillions of dollars on buybacks across the 2010s (over 70% of profits in his sample), a
    resource allocation whose long-term investment effects are still argued. The cautionary
    mechanism-design case is Wells Fargo (2016): aggressive cross-selling targets produced
    millions of unauthorised accounts — employees did what the metric asked, and the firm paid
    a settlement of about 3 billion dollars (2020). Governance theory's name for the failure is
    Goodhart's law: when a measure becomes a target, it stops measuring. The corrective tools
    are vesting periods, metric diversity and clawbacks — plus the older motivation insight
    (Herzberg) that recognition and meaningful work drive behaviour that no bonus formula
    captures.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Empire building, theorised.</strong> Jensen's free-cash-flow hypothesis (1986) names
    the classic failure mode: firms generating cash well beyond good investment opportunities
    face managers who would rather expand — acquisitions, prestige projects, headcount — than
    return the money to owners, because the empire is the manager's utility and the payout is
    only the shareholder's. The disciplining answers are governance in miniature: debt (which
    contracts future discretion), dividends and buybacks (contracted cash exits), and the
    threat of takeover. Jensen's own target was the oil industry of the early 1980s —
    cash-rich firms drilling anyway — and the leveraged-buyout wave that followed was the
    market's attempt to take the discretionary money back.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the board runs the company." Boards <em>oversee</em>;
    executives run. A board that starts managing operations has usually already failed at its
    actual jobs — hiring and firing the CEO, approving strategy and major transactions, and
    overseeing risk and audit — because those require information and courage, not meeting time.
  </p>

  <h2>Organisation and management: the board's machinery</h2>
  <p>
    Governance is organised into structures that make accountability practical. One-tier boards
    (US, UK) mix executives and independents; two-tier boards (Germany, Netherlands) separate a
    management board (Vorstand) from a supervisory board (Aufsichtsrat) — with German
    codetermination (1976 Act) reserving half the supervisory seats of large companies for
    employee representatives, the world's strongest worker voice at board level. Within
    one-tier systems, the real machinery is committees: audit (financial integrity, external
    auditor relationship), remuneration (incentive design), nomination (board composition) —
    each with independence requirements that codes specify. Management questions ride on the
    same frame: span of control, delegation of authority and decision rights (who decides,
    who consults — RAPID/DACI) are governance below board level. The design evidence:
    CEO/chair separation (recommended by the UK Corporate Governance Code) and genuinely
    independent audit committees correlate with fewer restatements; the correlations are
    modest, because structure only works when the people in it are willing to say no.
  </p>
  <p>
    The enforcement philosophies differ and the comparison teaches: Britain's Corporate
    Governance Code runs on <em>comply or explain</em> — listing rules with principles
    (board leadership, remuneration, audit) that companies may depart from if they explain
    why to the market, betting that investor scrutiny beats prescription. The US model
    (Sarbanes–Oxley, exchange listing standards) legislates specific duties with regulators
    and criminal liability behind them. Germany's two-tier statutory structure removes some
    choices entirely. None is clearly superior in outcomes; each reflects a national theory
    of who enforces governance — markets, states or stakeholders — and companies listing
    across systems must satisfy all three, which is why cross-listed firms carry the heaviest
    governance apparatus of all.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The shareholder voice, mechanised.</strong> Ownership is now institutional —
    the three largest index managers (BlackRock, Vanguard, State Street) together hold on
    the order of a fifth of typical S&P 500 votes — so engagement runs through
    <em>proxy voting</em>: annual meetings where directors are elected, auditors appointed
    and (since Dodd-Frank, 2010) executive pay is put to an advisory "say on pay" vote.
    Stewardship codes (the UK's, 2010; now emulated internationally) oblige institutional
    investors to engage actively rather than exit quietly. The open tension: passive funds
    cannot sell without un-indexing, so their governance power (votes, engagement) is
    decoupled from their economic exposure — the biggest unsolved design question in
    modern ownership.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "say on pay is the same everywhere — a symbolic vote
    shareholders can ignore." The regimes differ in legal force: the US vote (Dodd-Frank, 2010)
    is advisory, but Britain escalated to a binding statutory vote on remuneration policy from
    October 2013 (the Enterprise and Regulatory Reform Act 2013) — a pay policy needs
    shareholder approval to operate at all. The design lesson is mechanism design's again: an
    advisory vote produces information, a binding one produces bargaining — and boards now
    negotiate pay packages with proxy advisers before the meeting rather than after.
  </p>

  <h2>Recruitment, selection and training: who gets power, and how they learn it</h2>
  <p>
    Governance failures are usually appointment failures with a time lag: the wrong CEO
    selected, the compliant director retained, the auditor chosen for friendliness. The
    recruitment machinery tries to prevent this: nomination committees with published skills
    matrices, board evaluation (increasingly external), term limits and refreshment — because
    directors who owe their seats to the CEO are structurally compromised (the "grey director"
    problem). Selection evidence favours structured evaluation over impressions, exactly as in
    employee hiring: reference depth, competence in the company's actual risks. Training is the
    neglected half: new directors get induction on fiduciary duties, but the <em>tone at the
    top</em> — the ethical culture that Enron's "smartest guys" star culture corroded, where
    dissent was career-risk — is set by what leadership models and rewards, which is a training
    question. Whistleblowing channels belong in the same system: they are the recruitment of
    bad news, and their suppression is the signature of the governance failures below.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The external market for control.</strong> Appointment failure has an external
    corrector: the market for corporate control (Manne, 1965) — a badly governed firm's share
    price invites a bidder who will replace the management that wasted it. The defensive
    countermeasures are governance instruments in their own right: poison pills (rights plans
    diluting any acquirer, validated by Delaware's <em>Moran v. Household International</em>,
    1985), staggered boards and dual-class structures. The empirical record keeps the debate
    honest: hostile takeovers do discipline managers, pills do entrench them, and Delaware's
    repeated accommodation of management defences is the standing case study in whom company
    law actually serves — the market corrects; the law decides how often it is allowed to.
  </p>

  <h2>Internal and external communication: disclosure as control</h2>
  <p>
    Communication is governance's information system, in both directions. Internally, escalation
    matters more than announcement: Boeing's 737 MAX disaster (two crashes, 346 deaths, a 2.5-billion-dollar
    settlement in 2021) was an information failure — engineers' concerns about the MCAS
    software did not reach — or were not heard by — decision-makers, amid cost and schedule
    pressure. Cultures that punish bad news manufacture surprises; governance codes increasingly
    require boards to assess culture precisely for this reason. Externally, disclosure is the
    owners' information channel: annual reports, proxy statements, regulated
    announcements — with rules (the US's Reg FD, 2000) ensuring material information reaches all
    investors at once, not favoured analysts. Volkswagen's Dieselgate (2015: software cheating
    emissions tests on roughly 11 million vehicles; costs exceeding €30 billion) is the combined
    case: a fear-based internal communication culture plus a deliberate external deception —
    the two governance systems failing as one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "shareholders own the company." They own <em>shares</em> —
    claims on residual income and votes on defined matters — while the company is a separate
    legal person owning its own assets (a point of company law since <em>Salomon v
    Salomon</em>, 1897). The distinction is why governance is needed at all: owners cannot
    simply instruct the machine they partly own; they must appoint, incentivise and monitor it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "governance codes are laws, so compliance is compliance."
    They are layered: statute (company law, securities law) binds; listing rules carry
    sanctions; codes mostly exhort (comply or explain); best-practice guidance merely
    informs. A company can be fully "compliant" with every code while its governance fails —
    Enron's board checked boxes too — which is why the field evaluates governance by
    behaviour and outcomes (restatements, related-party dealings, auditor changes), not by
    documents.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Scandals as the curriculum.</strong> The regulatory corpus is a scandal map:
    Enron's special-purpose entities and mark-to-market abuse (2001) destroyed Arthur Andersen
    and produced Sarbanes–Oxley (2002) — auditor independence, CEO certification of accounts,
    internal-controls testing under section 404. The financial crisis (2008) produced
    say-on-pay and proxy access (Dodd-Frank, 2010). WeWork (2019) priced founder control in
    real time: dual-class shares with 20 votes for the founder against a 47-billion-dollar private
    valuation collapsing to roughly 8 billion within months of the IPO's withdrawal. Each
    reform is a patch; each next scandal tests the patches — which is why governance history is
    taught through failures, the way aviation safety is.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Enron's anatomy, one more time.</strong> The textbook case repays close reading:
    a gas pipeline company reinvented as "the world's leading company" (Fortune's most
    innovative firm six years running), whose special-purpose entities (the "Raptors" and
    LJM partnerships, run by CFO Andrew Fastow) moved debt and losses off the balance sheet
    while mark-to-market accounting booked projected profits from decade-long contracts as
    current earnings. The board waived its own ethics code to approve the partnerships; the
    auditor, Arthur Andersen, both consulted for and audited the firm — the conflict of
    interest SOX's auditor-independence rules exist to break. When the recursion collapsed
    (December 2001: at the time the largest bankruptcy in US history), roughly 20,000
    employees lost jobs and pensions largely invested in the company's own shares. Every
    subsequent rule — certification, internal controls, auditor rotation debates — is a
    patch on a named hole in this case.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The stakeholder debate, operationalised.</strong> Shareholder primacy (maximise
    owner value — Friedman's position) versus stakeholder governance (employees, customers,
    community counted) is usually argued as slogans; the operational question is measurement
    and voice. Stakeholders without enforceable voice are statements, not governance — which is
    why codetermination (binding employee seats), stewardship codes (institutional investors
    obliged to engage) and ESG-linked pay are the real battlefield. The measurement problem —
    stakeholder outcomes lack prices — is the honest frontier of the debate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Dual-class shares and founder control: innovation shield
    or accountability bypass (exchanges keep listing them; index providers disagree)? Activist
    investors: governance's pest control or short-termism's vanguard (the evidence is genuinely
    mixed)? And ESG disclosure: does mandated sustainability reporting change behaviour, or just
    reporting? Each is live in the journals and in the 2020s' rule-making — governance's
    frontier is empirical, not rhetorical.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Jensen & Meckling, JFE (1976); Sarbanes–Oxley Act (2002); Bebchuk & Weisbach, "The State of Corporate Governance Research" (2010).</p>
</TopicPage>
